# Inspect RequestSeal


This notebook installs RequestSeal 0.2.0 from Hex. It checks the module's public
function exports and actual runtime. Protocol verification is exercised by the
library's published-vector tests; this notebook checks installation and prerequisites.
Use the pinned Elixir/OTP runtime below. No provider key is required.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:request_seal, "~> 0.2.0"}])
```

## Inspect actual capabilities

```elixir
Code.ensure_loaded!(RequestSeal)
"1.20.4" = System.version()
"29" = System.otp_release()
true = :eddsa in Keyword.fetch!(:crypto.supports(), :public_keys)
true = :ed25519 in Keyword.fetch!(:crypto.supports(), :curves)
{:docs_v1, _, :elixir, _, %{"en" => module_docs}, _, _} = Code.fetch_docs(RequestSeal)
true = is_binary(module_docs)
[sign: 3, sign: 4, verify: 3, verify_quorum: 3] = RequestSeal.__info__(:functions)
IO.puts("RequestSeal module loaded; pinned runtime and Ed25519 available")
```

The result proves installation and prerequisites only. See [testing and evidence](../docs/guides/testing.md)
for the distinction between these checks and protocol support. No filesystem path, secret,
private key, request body, or token is printed. Mix's own dependency cache remains available;
the repository gate removes its temporary exported scripts after execution.
